From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: A733 HS400 timing selection - 2x mode, CCLK_DIV 0, module = card

Selects the HS400 timing on the A733 eMMC. All four fields are one choice, not
four knobs, so they land together:

    NTSR MODE_SEL         stays 1     2x timing, as every other mode here
    NTSR HS400_NEW_SAM_EN cleared     the 1x-mode sample variant is not ours
    NTSR DAT_SAM_TIM_PHS  cleared     ditto (the vendor also runs both at 0)
    CLKDIV CCLK_DIV       0           UM: must be 0 while HS400_MD_EN is set
    module clock          = card      no DDR52-style doubling for HS400

The last two need saying, because the code did the opposite. CCLK_DIV ended up 1
because a single `div` variable carried two jobs: the doubled module clock (right
for DDR52) and the internal divider (a UM violation under HS400_MD_EN). Split
into `clkdiv` for the register and `div` for the rate bookkeeping. And HS400 is
taken out of the DDR52 doubling entirely: under the new timing mode the module
clock already equals the card clock and the controller derives the DDR edges
itself.

How this was established -- an A/B on one binary, not a vendor transplant:
the same Image and
dtb were booted twice, HS200 produced by deleting a single DT property in U-Boot
(fdt rm /soc/mmc@4022000 mmc-hs400-1_8v). HS200 read 8 MiB twice with an
identical md5 and no errors at 200 MHz; HS400 returned zero bytes. Eight of the
eleven controller registers were byte-identical between the two. CSDC = 6 and
HS400_MD_EN = 1 are forced by the mode, and HALF_START_BIT was cleared at runtime
in the same boot and changed nothing. What was left was NTSR MODE_SEL plus the
module/card ratio -- the pair this patch fixes.

An earlier version of this patch cleared MODE_SEL and doubled the module clock,
copying the vendor's 1x configuration. That is not wrong for the vendor: it is a
different driver with a different clock model. This driver sets use_new_timings
for this controller and puts NTSR into 2x at probe, so half a vendor
configuration grafted on produced a state neither stack has ever run. The
measurement above is what settled it.

Hardware: this is one of the five changes that together make HS400 work on the 8 GiB board
(read + write error-free, ~120 MB/s). None of the five is
sufficient alone.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
@@ -77,12 +77,13 @@
 #define SDXC_REG_EMMC_DSBD	0x10C /* eMMC DDR Start Bit Detect Control */
 
 /* HS400 data-strobe fields (A733 UM 11.3, V5P3X controller) */
 #define SDXC_HS400_MD_EN		BIT(31)	  /* EMMC_DSBD: HS400 mode enable */
 #define SDXC_HALF_START_BIT		BIT(0)	  /* EMMC_DSBD: half start-bit (eMMC>=4.5) */
 #define SDXC_HS400_NEW_SAM_EN		BIT(0)	  /* SD_NTSR: HS400 new sample */
+#define SDXC_NTSR_MODE_SEL		BIT(31)	  /* SD_NTSR: new sample/output timing */
 #define SDXC_NTSR_DAT_SAM_PH_MASK	(0x3 << 8)
 #define SDXC_NTSR_DAT_SAM_PH_HS400	(0x3 << 8) /* SD2 HS400: 0 degree */
 #define SDXC_CSDC_CRC_DET_MASK		0xf
 #define SDXC_CSDC_HS400			0x6	  /* CSDC: HS400 speed mode */
 #define SDXC_CSDC_OTHER			0x3	  /* CSDC: non-HS400 speed mode */
 #define SDXC_DS_DL_SW_MASK		0x3f	  /* DS_DL: 6-bit SW delay */
@@ -864,17 +865,25 @@
 	/* CRC-status detect = HS400 speed mode (UM SMHC_CSDC[3:0] = 0b110) */
 	rval = mmc_readl(host, REG_CSDC);
 	rval &= ~SDXC_CSDC_CRC_DET_MASK;
 	rval |= SDXC_CSDC_HS400;
 	mmc_writel(host, REG_CSDC, rval);
 
-	/* HS400 new sample method + 0-degree data sample phase (UM SMHC_NTSR) */
+	/*
+	 * HS400 timing selection (UM SMHC_NTSR + Table 11-9). Keep MODE_SEL set,
+	 * clear HS400_NEW_SAM_EN and the data sample phase: with NEW_SAM_EN set the
+	 * UM halves the *data drive* phase from 90 to 45 degrees
+	 * (SMHC_DRV_DL DAT_DRV_PH_SEL in HS400: 90 degrees with 0x5C[0] = 0,
+	 * 45 degrees with 0x5C[0] = 1), and the data drive phase is the
+	 * write path. The three fields plus CCLK_DIV are one timing choice,
+	 * not four knobs. The vendor stack, which writes this eMMC without
+	 * error, also runs the two cleared fields at 0.
+	 */
 	rval = mmc_readl(host, REG_SD_NTSR);
-	rval &= ~SDXC_NTSR_DAT_SAM_PH_MASK;
-	rval |= SDXC_NTSR_DAT_SAM_PH_HS400;
-	rval |= SDXC_HS400_NEW_SAM_EN;
+	rval &= ~(SDXC_NTSR_DAT_SAM_PH_MASK | SDXC_HS400_NEW_SAM_EN);
+	rval |= SDXC_NTSR_MODE_SEL;
 	mmc_writel(host, REG_SD_NTSR, rval);
 
 	/*
 	 * Data-strobe delay: enable the software delay line, load
 	 * host->ds_dl. Stays 0: at 150 MHz every delay reads back, the
 	 * sweep was dropped, and the UM prescribes no value.
@@ -899,13 +908,13 @@
 
 static int sunxi_mmc_clk_set_rate(struct sunxi_mmc_host *host,
 				  struct mmc_ios *ios)
 {
 	struct mmc_host *mmc = host->mmc;
 	long rate;
-	u32 rval, clock = ios->clock, div = 1;
+	u32 rval, clock = ios->clock, div = 1, clkdiv = 1;
 	int ret;
 
 	ret = sunxi_mmc_oclk_onoff(host, 0);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
@@ -921,20 +930,37 @@
 	 * mode, all DDR modes require a doubled module clock. HS400 is
 	 * 8-bit DDR and uses the same 2x module clock: silicon shows a
 	 * 200 MHz HS400 card (module 400 MHz, CCLK_DIV=0) sustains reads,
 	 * whereas a 4x module (800 MHz) is too fast and no data-strobe
 	 * delay locks. So HS400 shares the DDR52 divider here.
 	 */
-	if ((ios->timing == MMC_TIMING_MMC_DDR52 ||
-	     ios->timing == MMC_TIMING_MMC_HS400) &&
+	if (ios->timing == MMC_TIMING_MMC_DDR52 &&
 	    (host->use_new_timings ||
 	     ios->bus_width == MMC_BUS_WIDTH_8)) {
 		div = 2;
 		clock <<= 1;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * HS400 does NOT take the DDR52 doubling. Under the new timing mode
+	 * the module clock already equals the card clock and the controller
+	 * derives the DDR edges itself -- which is exactly how HS200 runs on
+	 * this board: 200 MHz module, 200 MHz card, NTSR MODE_SEL set, and
+	 * not a single data error. The A/B that established this is
+	 * the following: one
+	 * kernel, one card, HS200 versus HS400 with only the DT property
+	 * removed, eight of eleven controller registers byte-identical, and
+	 * the whole difference in the timing mode plus the module/card ratio.
+	 *
+	 * CCLK_DIV stays 0 regardless (it must be 0 while HS400_MD_EN is set,
+	 * UM SMHC_CLKDIV), which is what clkdiv = 1 encodes
+	 * here -- the register takes clkdiv - 1.
+	 */
+	if (ios->timing == MMC_TIMING_MMC_HS400)
+		clkdiv = 1;
+
 	if (host->use_new_timings && host->cfg->ccu_has_timings_switch) {
 		ret = sunxi_ccu_set_mmc_timing_mode(host->clk_mmc, true);
 		if (ret) {
 			dev_err(mmc_dev(mmc),
 				"error setting new timing mode\n");
 			return ret;
@@ -958,13 +984,13 @@
 		return ret;
 	}
 
 	/* set internal divider */
 	rval = mmc_readl(host, REG_CLKCR);
 	rval &= ~0xff;
-	rval |= div - 1;
+	rval |= clkdiv - 1;
 	mmc_writel(host, REG_CLKCR, rval);
 
 	/* update card clock rate to account for internal divider */
 	rate /= div;
 
 	/*
